// One-off: IB subject requirements for every programme whose ib_subjects was
// blank, plus a new ib_maths column for the maths filter. Checked 2026-10-26.
//
//   node tools/add-ib-subjects-2026-10-26.mjs
//
// Re-runnable. It writes ib_subjects and ib_maths (and last_verified) for the
// rows in NEW and REWRITE, ib_maths only for MATHS_ONLY, ib_points_min only
// where blank for POINTS, and never overwrites an ib_subjects someone else
// filled in: a NEW row whose cell is no longer blank stops the script.
//
// ib_maths lists the IB maths courses a programme accepts, joined with " · ":
// "AA HL · AA SL · AI HL · AI SL". "none" means no maths requirement, so every
// course qualifies. "?" means the official page does not say which IB course is
// enough; the filter never hides those. None is left after Groningen's IB table,
// Wageningen's 2027 eligibility list, RSM's IB page and Radboud's non-Dutch
// diploma page were read (all quoted in REWRITE or NEW). Grades stay in ib_subjects.
// Where an official page only says "Mathematics HL" (Danish tables), both AA HL
// and AI HL are listed; where it only names a level of "Mathematics", every
// course at that level is.
//
// How each country was read:
//
//   Netherlands — the university's own IB table: UvA's diploma finder, Leiden's
//     "Specific diploma requirements for science programmes 2027-2028" PDF,
//     Twente's IB page, Tilburg's maths-requirements page, VU's "IB Diploma
//     requirements 2027-2028" PDF, Groningen's IB diploma table, Wageningen's
//     2027 Diploma Eligibility List, RSM's IB page, Radboud's per-programme PDFs, Utrecht's
//     programme pages and MBLS PDF. A programme a university's list does not
//     name has no extra requirement where the list says so (UvA: "If your
//     programme is not listed below, no extra/alternative requirements apply").
//   Denmark — the programme's Danish subject levels, converted with the
//     university's own IB table (Aalborg's conversion image, SDU's "tjek krav"
//     page). Levels a table does not cover are left in Danish terms and say so.
//   Ireland — the course page's Leaving Certificate subject requirements,
//     converted with the CAO "Agreed Entry Requirements for EU/EFTA/UK" (Nov
//     2025) IB table, which every Irish university points EU applicants to:
//     "To compare the subject requirements in terms of Irish Leaving
//     Certificate as specified on the HEI's website, use the following table".
//     LC H1 = HL 7, H2/H3 = HL 6, H4/H5 = HL 5, H6 = HL 4, H7 = HL 3;
//     O1 = SL 7, O2/O3 = SL 6, O4/O5 = SL 5, O6 = SL 4. UCC's worked IB example
//     on its EU page applies exactly this table. English grades go in
//     english_requirement unless a course asks for a grade in English as a
//     subject. At NUI universities (UCD, UCC, Galway, Maynooth) Irish is waived
//     automatically for applicants "born outside Ireland (32 Counties) and ...
//     permanently resident outside Ireland" presenting other qualifications
//     (UCD Registry, minimum entry requirements page).
//   Italy — no school subjects are required anywhere; admission is by test or
//     application. The national rule for the IB Diploma (MUR circular
//     2026-2027, Allegato 1 §6: 24+ points, 12 of them at HL, TOK/CAS/EE
//     passed) is stated on every card. Bocconi and Cattolica have their own IB
//     pages, quoted on their rows.
//   Czechia — each programme's admission section was searched for any
//     maturita or IB subject condition; the only ones are exam waivers, which
//     are written out where they name IB subjects.
//
// Every row is filled. Graduate-entry courses say IB subjects don't apply;
// UL's two dance degrees and Cyber Security use UL's university-wide minimum
// and the common-entry route their pages point to; Trinity's Nutrition degree
// uses TU Dublin's TU870 page, which runs the joint course.

import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { parseCsv } from '../js/csv.js';

const DATA = new URL('../data/', import.meta.url);
const TODAY = '2026-10-26';
const esc = (v) => { const s = String(v ?? ''); return /[",\n]/.test(s) ? `"${s.replace(/"/g, '""')}"` : s; };
const COURSES = ['AA HL', 'AA SL', 'AI HL', 'AI SL'];

const url = new URL('programmes.csv', DATA);
const [head, ...rows] = parseCsv(readFileSync(url, 'utf8'));
// A row that points at another row's subjects ("see the main course page").
const COPY = (id) => ({ copy: id });

// Source wording shared by every entry of one university, quoted from the
// pages and documents it names. An entry's own source is its URL and quote,
// followed by the shared wording for its university.
